add({ name: "Sampalpuri Ikkat Saree", region: "Odisha", yearsFact: "Approximately 1,000+ years.",
  history: "Sambalpuri Ikkat sarees are a renowned traditional handloom textile from Sambalpur, celebrated for their unique tie-and-dye weaving technique and artistic designs. The craft has a history spanning several centuries and was developed by skilled weavers of the Odisha region. The distinctive Ikkat method involves carefully tying and dyeing yarns before weaving them to create beautiful geometric patterns and traditional motifs. Initially, these sarees were created using natural fibers and reflected the cultural stories, nature, and traditions of local communities. Over time, Sambalpuri Ikkat sarees gained recognition for their intricate craftsmanship, vibrant colors, and durability.",
  traditionalUsage: "Sambalpuri Ikkat sarees are handcrafted using the traditional tie-and-dye Ikkat technique with premium cotton or silk fabric, featuring intricate motifs and vibrant colors. They are ideal for festivals, weddings, cultural events, office wear, and special occasions due to their elegant designs and comfortable drape." });

add({ name: "Mangalgiri Printed Kalamkari", region: "Andhra Pradesh (Mangalagiri town, Palnadu district)", yearsFact: "Approximately 500+ years.",
  history: "Mangalgiri Printed Kalamkari sarees represent the beautiful blend of two traditional textile arts of India — the fine weaving of Mangalagiri and the ancient art of Kalamkari printing. Kalamkari has roots dating back centuries, with artisans using natural dyes and hand-painted or block-printing techniques to create detailed stories, motifs, and patterns on fabric. The Mangalgiri weaving tradition is known for its lightweight cotton fabric and distinctive weave, which became a perfect base for Kalamkari designs. Over time, artisans adapted traditional Kalamkari themes with modern prints, colors, and patterns to suit changing fashion preferences. These sarees evolved from temple and cultural textiles into elegant everyday and festive wear.",
  traditionalUsage: "Mangalgiri Printed Kalamkari sarees are crafted from lightweight Mangalgiri cotton fabric with traditional Kalamkari prints featuring artistic motifs, vibrant colors, and elegant patterns. They are suitable for daily wear, office wear, festivals, cultural events, and casual occasions due to their comfort and graceful appearance." });

add({ name: "Kalamkari Saree", region: "Andhra Pradesh, India (especially Srikalahasti and Machilipatnam regions)", yearsFact: "Approximately 500+ years.",
  history: "Kalamkari sarees are a traditional Indian textile art form known for their intricate hand-painted and block-printed designs. The word Kalamkari comes from the Persian words \"kalam\" meaning pen and \"kari\" meaning work, referring to the use of a pen-like tool to create detailed artwork. This craft has ancient roots in India, with its development closely associated with regions like Srikalahasti and Machilipatnam. Traditionally, artisans used natural dyes and created mythological stories, floral patterns, and nature-inspired motifs on cotton fabrics. Over centuries, Kalamkari sarees evolved through the influence of temple art, cultural traditions, and changing fashion trends.",
  traditionalUsage: "Kalamkari sarees are handcrafted using fine cotton or silk fabric with traditional hand-painted or block-printed Kalamkari artwork, featuring intricate motifs and natural-inspired designs. They are suitable for festivals, weddings, cultural events, office wear, and special occasions due to their artistic elegance." });

add({ name: "Kanchi Silk Pen Kalamkari", region: "Tamil Nadu (Kanchipuram) and Andhra Pradesh", yearsFact: "Approximately 400–500 years.",
  history: "Kanchi Silk Pen Kalamkari sarees represent the elegant fusion of two rich Indian textile traditions — the luxurious silk weaving of Kanchipuram and the artistic storytelling of Kalamkari. Kanchipuram silk weaving has a history of several centuries and is known for its rich texture, zari work, and traditional craftsmanship. Kalamkari, an ancient art form, developed through hand-painted techniques using a pen-like tool called a kalam to create detailed motifs. Artisans combined these two crafts to create sarees featuring intricate Kalamkari designs on premium silk fabric. Traditionally, the motifs were inspired by mythology, nature, temple art, and cultural stories.",
  traditionalUsage: "Kanchi Silk Pen Kalamkari sarees are crafted from premium Kanchipuram silk fabric with intricate hand-painted Kalamkari artwork and traditional zari weaving, offering a rich and elegant finish. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, religious ceremonies, cultural events, and grand celebrations due to their luxurious look." });

add({ name: "Organza Lucknowi", region: "Lucknow", yearsFact: "Approximately 200–300 years, with the embroidery tradition flourishing during the Nawabs of Awadh in the 18th century.",
  history: "Organza Lucknowi sarees are a graceful blend of delicate organza fabric and the traditional Chikankari embroidery of Lucknow. Chikankari is an ancient embroidery craft that flourished under the patronage of the Mughal Empire and became a symbol of elegance and fine craftsmanship. Traditionally, artisans hand-embroidered floral and geometric motifs on soft cotton fabrics using intricate needlework. Over time, this exquisite embroidery was adapted to lightweight organza fabric, creating sarees with a sheer texture and refined appearance. Skilled artisans combined traditional Chikankari techniques with modern designs, colors, and embellishments to suit contemporary tastes.",
  traditionalUsage: "Organza Lucknowi sarees are crafted from lightweight organza fabric adorned with traditional Lucknowi Chikankari embroidery, offering a sheer texture, elegant drape, and intricate handcrafted designs. They are perfect for weddings, festivals, parties, cultural events, and special occasions." });

add({ name: "Kora Organza", region: "West Bengal", yearsFact: "Approximately 200–300 years.",
  history: "Kora Organza sarees are a timeless part of India's rich weaving tradition, admired for their lightweight texture, sheer elegance, and fine craftsmanship. Organza weaving was introduced to India centuries ago and gradually became popular among skilled artisans for creating graceful and luxurious fabrics. Kora Organza is known for its crisp texture, transparent appearance, and delicate finish, making it a preferred choice for elegant attire. Traditionally, these sarees were enhanced with hand embroidery, zari work, and woven motifs inspired by nature and Indian art. Over time, weavers combined traditional techniques with modern designs, vibrant colors, and contemporary patterns to suit changing fashion trends.",
  traditionalUsage: "Kora Organza sarees are crafted from fine Kora Organza fabric, known for its lightweight, sheer texture and crisp finish, often enhanced with elegant embroidery, zari work, or printed designs. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, parties, cultural events, and formal occasions due to their graceful drape and sophisticated appearance." });

add({ name: "Pure Organza", region: "West Bengal", yearsFact: "Approximately 200–300 years.",
  history: "Pure Organza sarees are a symbol of elegance and fine craftsmanship in India's rich textile tradition. Organza fabric was originally introduced through silk trade routes and was later adopted by Indian weavers, who refined it using traditional weaving techniques. Known for its lightweight, sheer texture, and crisp finish, Pure Organza quickly became a preferred fabric for creating graceful and luxurious sarees. Skilled artisans enhanced these sarees with intricate embroidery, zari work, hand-painted art, and woven motifs inspired by Indian culture and nature. Over time, Pure Organza sarees evolved with modern colors, floral prints, and contemporary designs while preserving their classic appeal.",
  traditionalUsage: "Pure Organza sarees are crafted from premium pure organza fabric, known for its lightweight, sheer texture, crisp finish, and elegant appearance, often featuring embroidery, zari work, or printed designs. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, parties, receptions, cultural events, and formal occasions due to their graceful drape." });

add({ name: "Tissue Organza", region: "West Bengal", yearsFact: "Approximately 200–300 years.",
  history: "Tissue Organza sarees are a beautiful blend of traditional Indian weaving techniques and modern textile innovation. These sarees combine the lightweight transparency of organza with the subtle metallic sheen of tissue fabric, creating a rich and elegant appearance. Indian weavers adopted this unique fabric combination to produce luxurious sarees suitable for festive and ceremonial wear. Traditionally, Tissue Organza sarees were enhanced with zari work, intricate embroidery, and woven motifs inspired by nature, temples, and cultural heritage. Over time, artisans introduced vibrant colors, floral patterns, and contemporary designs while preserving the elegance of traditional craftsmanship.",
  traditionalUsage: "Tissue Organza sarees are crafted from a premium blend of tissue and organza fabric, featuring a lightweight, sheer texture with a subtle metallic sheen, often enhanced with zari work, embroidery, or elegant prints. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, receptions, parties, and cultural events due to their luxurious appearance." });

add({ name: "Semi Silk Sarees", region: "Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, West Bengal", yearsFact: "Approximately 100+ years.",
  history: "Semi Silk sarees emerged as a modern adaptation of India's rich silk weaving tradition, offering the beauty of silk at a more affordable price. Inspired by the luxurious silk sarees worn for centuries, artisans developed semi silk fabrics by blending silk with other fibers while maintaining an elegant appearance. These sarees became popular because they combined the sheen and graceful drape of silk with improved durability and easier maintenance. Over time, Indian weavers introduced traditional motifs, zari work, and regional weaving styles into semi silk sarees, preserving cultural craftsmanship.",
  traditionalUsage: "Semi Silk sarees are crafted from a premium silk-blend fabric that offers the rich look of silk with added durability, comfort, and a smooth, elegant finish. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, office wear, family functions, cultural events, and special occasions due to their versatile style and graceful drape." });

add({ name: "Semi Patola", region: "Gujarat", yearsFact: "Approximately 900+ years (Patola weaving tradition); semi-Patola styles developed as modern adaptations.",
  history: "Semi Patola sarees are inspired by the famous Patola weaving tradition of Patan, which has been admired for centuries for its intricate double Ikat designs and vibrant colors. Traditional Patola sarees were handwoven by skilled artisans and were once worn mainly by royalty and wealthy families because of their exceptional craftsmanship. To make the beauty of Patola designs more affordable and accessible, artisans developed Semi Patola sarees using silk-blend or other quality fabrics while retaining the classic patterns. Over time, these sarees evolved with modern weaving techniques, contemporary color combinations, and innovative motifs.",
  traditionalUsage: "Semi Patola sarees are crafted from premium silk-blend or art silk fabric with traditional Patola-inspired Ikat patterns, offering a rich look, vibrant colors, and a graceful drape. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, family functions, cultural events, and special occasions due to their elegant and timeless appeal." });

add({ name: "Semi Mysore Silk", region: "Karnataka, India (Mysore region)", yearsFact: "Approximately 100+ years.",
  history: "Semi Mysore Silk sarees are inspired by the renowned silk weaving tradition of Mysuru, which has been celebrated for its elegance and fine craftsmanship for over a century. The original Mysore Silk sarees gained recognition for their pure silk fabric, smooth texture, and exquisite zari borders. To make this classic style more affordable and accessible, artisans introduced Semi Mysore Silk sarees using silk-blend fabrics while preserving the traditional look. Over time, these sarees evolved with modern weaving techniques, vibrant color combinations, and contemporary designs.",
  traditionalUsage: "Semi Mysore Silk sarees are crafted from premium silk-blend fabric with a smooth texture, elegant sheen, and traditional zari borders, offering the classic look of Mysore Silk at an affordable price. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, religious ceremonies, office wear, family functions, and special occasions." });

add({ name: "Semi Crepe", region: "Gujarat (especially Surat)", yearsFact: "Approximately 50–100 years (widely produced since the mid-20th century with the rise of synthetic textiles).",
  history: "Semi Crepe sarees are a modern interpretation of India's rich saree tradition, combining traditional elegance with the comfort of contemporary fabrics. Crepe fabric, known for its soft texture, slight crinkled surface, and graceful drape, became popular in India through evolving textile techniques and global fabric influences. Indian artisans adapted crepe weaving methods to create sarees that were lightweight, comfortable, and easy to maintain. Semi Crepe sarees were developed by blending crepe with other fibers to offer durability, affordability, and a luxurious appearance. Over time, these sarees evolved with vibrant prints, embroidery, digital designs, and traditional motifs inspired by Indian culture.",
  traditionalUsage: "Semi Crepe sarees are crafted from a soft crepe-blend fabric with a smooth texture, elegant fall, and lightweight feel, often featuring stylish prints, patterns, or embellishments. They are suitable for daily wear, office wear, parties, festivals, and special occasions due to their comfort, versatility, and graceful appearance." });

add({ name: "Art Silk Saree", region: "Gujarat (especially Surat)", yearsFact: "Approximately 100+ years.",
  history: "Art Silk sarees are a modern development in India's long-standing silk textile tradition, created to provide the luxurious appearance of silk at an affordable price. With the growth of textile technology, synthetic fibers such as rayon and other man-made materials were developed to imitate the smooth texture and shine of natural silk. Indian artisans adapted these fibers to create elegant sarees that combined traditional designs with modern convenience. Over time, Art Silk sarees gained popularity due to their lightweight feel, vibrant colors, durability, and easy maintenance. Weavers incorporated traditional motifs, zari borders, prints, and embroidery inspired by classic Indian saree styles.",
  traditionalUsage: "Art Silk sarees are crafted from high-quality synthetic silk-blend fabric, designed to provide a silk-like shine, smooth texture, lightweight feel, and elegant drape. They are suitable for weddings, festivals, office wear, parties, cultural events, and daily occasions due to their affordability and versatile style." });

add({ name: "Semi Soft Kanchi Silk", region: "Kanchipuram", yearsFact: "Approximately 400–500 years.",
  history: "Semi Soft Kanchi Silk sarees are inspired by the rich weaving heritage of Kanchipuram, which is renowned for its traditional silk sarees and skilled craftsmanship. Kanchipuram silk weaving has a history of several centuries, with artisans creating luxurious sarees known for their rich texture, zari work, and intricate designs. To make this heritage style more comfortable and accessible, Semi Soft Kanchi Silk sarees were developed with a softer silk-blend fabric and lightweight feel. These sarees preserve traditional elements such as temple borders, classic motifs, and elegant color combinations. Over time, weavers introduced modern designs, vibrant shades, and contemporary patterns to suit changing fashion trends. Improvements in textile techniques enhanced the softness, durability, and ease of draping while maintaining the traditional charm.",
  traditionalUsage: "Semi Soft Kanchi Silk sarees are crafted from soft silk-blend fabric inspired by traditional Kanchipuram silk, featuring elegant zari borders, rich colors, and a smooth, comfortable drape. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, religious ceremonies, family functions, and special occasions due to their luxurious yet lightweight feel." });

add({ name: "Semi Tusser", region: "Jharkhand, Bihar, Odisha, Chhattisgarh, West Bengal", yearsFact: "Approximately 1,000+ years (Tussar silk tradition in India); semi-tussar sarees became popular as a blended fabric in the 20th century.",
  history: "Semi Tussar sarees are inspired by India's ancient Tussar silk weaving tradition, which is known for its natural texture, rich appearance, and earthy elegance. Tussar silk has been produced in India for centuries and was valued for its unique golden sheen and connection to traditional handloom craftsmanship. To make this heritage fabric more affordable and accessible, artisans developed Semi Tussar sarees using silk blends and other fibers while maintaining the charm of Tussar silk. Over time, these sarees evolved with new colors, patterns, printed designs, and contemporary weaving techniques.",
  traditionalUsage: "Semi Tussar sarees are crafted from a silk-blend fabric inspired by traditional Tussar silk, featuring a natural texture, elegant sheen, and beautiful woven or printed designs. They are suitable for weddings, festivals, office wear, cultural events, and special occasions due to their graceful appearance and comfortable drape." });

add({ name: "Banarasi Semi Silk", region: "Varanasi", yearsFact: "Approximately 400–500 years.",
  history: "Banarasi Semi Silk sarees are inspired by the famous Banarasi weaving tradition of Varanasi, one of India's oldest and most celebrated centers of silk craftsmanship. For centuries, Banarasi sarees have been admired for their luxurious silk, intricate zari work, and finely woven floral and Mughal-inspired motifs. To make this timeless style more affordable and practical, artisans introduced Banarasi Semi Silk sarees using silk-blend fabrics while preserving the traditional elegance. Over time, these sarees evolved with modern weaving techniques, vibrant colors, and contemporary patterns that appealed to a wider audience. Skilled weavers continued to incorporate classic brocade designs, paisleys, and decorative borders into semi silk fabrics. Advances in textile technology improved the durability, comfort, and affordability of these sarees without compromising their rich appearance.",
  traditionalUsage: "Banarasi Semi Silk sarees are crafted from a silk-blend fabric featuring traditional Banarasi-inspired zari work, brocade motifs, rich colors, and an elegant silk-like finish. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, religious functions, family celebrations, and special occasions due to their luxurious appearance and graceful drape." });

add({ name: "Semi Jamdhani", region: "West Bengal", yearsFact: "Approximately 400–500 years.",
  history: "Semi Jamdani sarees are inspired by the historic Jamdani weaving tradition of India, known for its delicate patterns, fine craftsmanship, and artistic motifs. The Jamdani technique has roots dating back centuries and flourished in the Bengal region, where skilled weavers created intricate designs using handloom methods. Traditionally, Jamdani sarees were highly valued for their lightweight texture and detailed floral, geometric, and nature-inspired motifs. To make this heritage style more accessible, artisans developed Semi Jamdani sarees using blended fabrics while preserving the beauty of traditional designs. Over time, these sarees evolved with new colors, patterns, and modern weaving techniques to match changing fashion trends.",
  traditionalUsage: "Semi Jamdani sarees are crafted from a lightweight silk-blend or cotton-blend fabric featuring traditional Jamdani-inspired woven motifs, delicate patterns, and elegant designs. They are suitable for festivals, office wear, cultural events, casual gatherings, and special occasions due to their comfortable drape and artistic appeal." });

add({ name: "Maheshwari Silk", region: "Maheshwar", yearsFact: "Approximately 250–300 years.",
  history: "Maheshwari Silk sarees are a traditional Indian handloom treasure that originated from Maheshwar and are known for their elegant texture, lightweight feel, and distinctive designs. The weaving tradition of Maheshwari sarees dates back to the 18th century and is associated with the patronage of Ahilyabai Holkar, who encouraged local textile craftsmanship. Originally woven in cotton, Maheshwari sarees later evolved with silk blends to create richer and more luxurious variations. Skilled artisans developed signature features such as striped patterns, reversible borders, and motifs inspired by temples and nature.",
  traditionalUsage: "Maheshwari Silk sarees are handcrafted from a silk-blend fabric with traditional Maheshwari weaving, featuring elegant borders, fine textures, and classic motifs inspired by heritage designs. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, cultural events, office wear, and special occasions due to their lightweight feel and graceful drape." });

add({ name: "Mangalgiri Saree", region: "Andhra Pradesh (Mangalagiri town, Palnadu district)", yearsFact: "Approximately 500+ years.",
  history: "Mangalgiri sarees are a traditional handloom textile from Mangalagiri, known for their fine cotton texture, durability, and elegant simplicity. The weaving tradition of Mangalagiri has existed for centuries and was developed by skilled local weavers using traditional handloom techniques. These sarees were originally valued for their lightweight fabric, comfort, and suitability for the warm climate of the region. A distinctive feature of Mangalgiri sarees is their plain body, vibrant colors, and zari or woven borders that add a touch of elegance.",
  traditionalUsage: "Mangalgiri sarees are handcrafted from fine-quality cotton fabric with traditional handloom weaving, featuring lightweight texture, vibrant colors, and elegant zari borders. They are suitable for daily wear, office wear, festivals, cultural events, and casual occasions due to their comfort, breathability, and graceful look." });

add({ name: "Assam Silk", region: "Assam", yearsFact: "Approximately 1,000+ years.",
  history: "Assam Silk originates from Assam, India, and has a rich weaving heritage dating back over 1,000 years. The tradition flourished during the Ahom dynasty, when skilled weavers developed exquisite silk fabrics for royal families and ceremonial occasions. Assam is especially known for its three varieties of silk — Muga, Eri, and Pat silk, with Muga silk being famous for its natural golden sheen and exceptional durability. Traditionally woven on handlooms, Assam silk sarees showcase intricate motifs inspired by nature, Assamese culture, and traditional craftsmanship.",
  traditionalUsage: "Assam Silk sarees are handcrafted from premium Assam silk fabrics such as Muga, Eri, or Pat silk, known for their natural sheen, rich texture, durability, and traditional motifs. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, cultural events, religious occasions, and special celebrations due to their elegant appearance and heritage appeal." });

add({ name: "Chanderi Saree", region: "Madhya Pradesh (Chanderi town, Ashoknagar district)", yearsFact: "Approximately 1,000+ years.",
  history: "Chanderi sarees are a celebrated Indian handloom textile originating from Chanderi, known for their sheer texture, lightweight feel, and elegant appearance. The weaving tradition of Chanderi dates back several centuries and was influenced by royal patronage, especially during the reign of various Indian kingdoms. Traditionally, these sarees were woven using fine cotton yarns, later incorporating silk to create richer and more luxurious fabrics. Chanderi sarees are recognized for their glossy texture, delicate zari borders, and motifs inspired by nature, art, and traditional designs. Skilled artisans preserved ancient handloom techniques while adapting to changing fashion preferences.",
  traditionalUsage: "Chanderi sarees are handcrafted from fine cotton, silk, or silk-blend fabric, known for their lightweight texture, sheer finish, elegant zari borders, and delicate woven motifs. They are suitable for festivals, weddings, office wear, cultural events, and special occasions due to their graceful drape and comfortable feel." });

add({ name: "Dupion Silk", region: "Karnataka", yearsFact: "Approximately 1,000–2,000 years.",
  history: "Dupion Silk sarees are a luxurious textile known for their rich texture, crisp feel, and natural sheen, forming part of India's long-standing silk weaving tradition. Dupion silk is created using uneven silk fibers, which give the fabric its distinctive slubbed texture and elegant appearance. Indian artisans adapted this silk to create graceful sarees that combine traditional craftsmanship with modern fashion sensibilities. Over time, Dupion Silk sarees gained popularity for their durability, vibrant colors, and sophisticated finish. Weavers enhanced these sarees with zari borders, embroidery, woven patterns, and traditional Indian motifs.",
  traditionalUsage: "Dupion Silk sarees are crafted from premium Dupion silk fabric with a distinctive textured surface, natural sheen, rich colors, and elegant designs enhanced with zari, embroidery, or woven patterns. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, receptions, cultural events, and formal occasions due to their luxurious look and graceful drape." });

add({ name: "Kanchi Silk", region: "Tamil Nadu (Kanchipuram town, Chengalpattu district)", yearsFact: "Approximately 400–500 years.",
  history: "Kanchi Silk sarees, also known as Kanchipuram Silk sarees, are among India's most renowned traditional handloom textiles, originating from Kanchipuram. The weaving tradition of Kanchipuram silk dates back several centuries and is deeply connected with the skilled craftsmanship of local silk weavers. Traditionally, these sarees were created using pure mulberry silk and real zari, making them symbols of luxury and elegance. The designs often feature temple borders, floral patterns, mythological motifs, and traditional South Indian artistic elements. Over time, Kanchi Silk sarees gained popularity among royalty and became an essential part of weddings and festive celebrations.",
  traditionalUsage: "Kanchi Silk sarees are handcrafted from premium pure silk fabric with traditional zari work, rich colors, intricate borders, and classic motifs that reflect South Indian weaving heritage. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, religious ceremonies, cultural events, and grand celebrations due to their luxurious texture and elegant drape." });

add({ name: "Rangkat Silk Saree", region: "Gujarat and Rajasthan", yearsFact: "Approximately 200–300 years.",
  history: "Rangkat Silk sarees are a traditional Indian textile known for their beautiful combination of multiple colors, rich texture, and unique dyeing techniques. The term \"Rangkat\" refers to the process of blending or layering different shades of colors on silk fabric to create a distinctive multicolored effect. This craft developed through the creativity of Indian weavers who experimented with traditional silk weaving and dyeing methods. Inspired by India's rich silk heritage, Rangkat sarees evolved with intricate patterns, vibrant hues, and elegant designs. Over time, artisans incorporated traditional motifs, zari work, and contemporary patterns to enhance their beauty. Improvements in dyeing and weaving techniques helped create more refined and durable Rangkat Silk sarees.",
  traditionalUsage: "Rangkat Silk sarees are crafted from premium silk fabric featuring unique multicolored dye effects, rich textures, vibrant shades, and elegant traditional designs. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, cultural events, receptions, and special occasions due to their luxurious appearance and graceful drape." });

add({ name: "Handloom Chanderi Silk", region: "Chanderi, Madhya Pradesh", yearsFact: "Chanderi weaving traditions date back around 1,000 years.",
  history: "Handloom Chanderi Silk sarees are a celebrated Indian textile tradition originating from Chanderi, known for their lightweight texture, sheer elegance, and fine craftsmanship. The weaving heritage of Chanderi dates back several centuries and flourished under the patronage of royal families who appreciated its delicate beauty. Traditionally, artisans used fine cotton yarns, later blending silk to create richer and more luxurious Chanderi Silk sarees. These sarees became famous for their glossy finish, lightweight feel, zari borders, and delicate motifs inspired by nature and traditional art. Skilled handloom weavers preserved ancient techniques while adapting designs to changing fashion preferences.",
  traditionalUsage: "Handloom Chanderi Silk sarees are crafted using fine silk fabric with traditional handloom weaving, featuring a lightweight texture, sheer finish, elegant zari borders, and delicate woven motifs. They are suitable for weddings, festivals, office wear, cultural events, and special occasions due to their graceful drape and sophisticated appeal." });

add({ name: "Katan Silk Saree", region: "Uttar Pradesh (especially Varanasi/Banaras region)", yearsFact: "Approximately 400–500 years.",
  history: "Katan Silk sarees are a traditional Indian silk textile known for their pure silk texture, smooth finish, and luxurious appearance. The term \"Katan\" refers to a fine silk fabric created by twisting and weaving silk threads, resulting in a strong and elegant material. This weaving tradition has deep roots in India's silk heritage and became especially prominent in the famous weaving centers of Varanasi. Traditionally, Katan Silk was used to create rich sarees with intricate zari work, floral motifs, and classic Indian designs. Over time, skilled artisans refined weaving techniques and introduced new patterns, colors, and contemporary styles. The popularity of Katan Silk sarees grew due to their softness, durability, and graceful drape.",
  traditionalUsage: "Katan Silk sarees are crafted from fine pure silk fabric with a smooth texture, rich sheen, elegant drape, and traditional designs often enhanced with zari work and woven motifs. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, cultural events, religious ceremonies, and grand occasions due to their luxurious look and timeless appeal." });

add({ name: "Kora Silk Sarees", region: "Varanasi", yearsFact: "Approximately 400–500 years.",
  history: "Kora Silk sarees are a traditional Indian textile known for their lightweight texture, sheer elegance, and delicate silk craftsmanship. The term \"Kora\" refers to the fine, raw silk texture that gives these sarees a natural sheen and crisp appearance. This weaving tradition developed through India's rich silk heritage, where skilled artisans created lightweight fabrics for graceful and luxurious garments. Traditionally, Kora Silk sarees were enhanced with zari work, woven motifs, and intricate patterns inspired by nature and cultural art. Over time, weavers introduced new colors, designs, and contemporary styles while preserving the traditional charm of the fabric.",
  traditionalUsage: "Kora Silk sarees are crafted from fine Kora silk fabric with a lightweight, sheer texture, natural sheen, crisp finish, and elegant designs featuring zari work or woven motifs. They are suitable for weddings, festivals, cultural events, receptions, and special occasions due to their graceful drape and sophisticated appeal." });

add({ name: "Mashru Silk Sarees", region: "Gujarat (especially Patan and other weaving centers of Gujarat)", yearsFact: "Approximately 500–700 years.",
  history: "Mashru Silk sarees are a traditional Indian textile known for their unique silk-and-cotton blend, vibrant colors, and rich cultural heritage. The word \"Mashru\" is derived from the Persian term meaning \"permitted\" and refers to a fabric historically created to combine the luxury of silk with the comfort of cotton. This weaving tradition flourished in regions such as Gujarat and Karnataka, where skilled artisans developed distinctive patterns and techniques. Traditionally, Mashru fabrics were favored by royalty and communities who appreciated their glossy silk exterior and comfortable cotton reverse side.",
  traditionalUsage: "Mashru Silk sarees are crafted from a traditional silk-cotton blend fabric with a smooth silk surface, comfortable cotton feel, vibrant colors, and elegant woven patterns. They are ideal for weddings, festivals, cultural events, office wear, and special occasions due to their unique texture and graceful appearance." });

